{"id":"35d9aeb9-ba53-433a-b536-fb9f145664a3","object":"chat.completion","created":1793333481,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n```python\nprint(search_code(\"LOG_MAX_MESSAGE\", [\"deepinfer/environ.py\", \"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\"], 10))\nh = \"5b8f8d9947de692353609c1fc2851097d24f9b6ba6004dce1dbfa394dc6d3ec8\"\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 'import datetime\\nimport logging', 'import copy\\nimport datetime\\nimport logging', h)\nprint(r.get(\"rc\", r))\nh = r.get(\"file_hash\", h) if isinstance(r, dict) else h\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>","reasoning_content":"The progress says environ.py already changed (changed_files includes environ.py). Next requirement: remaining source changes: stats_logger.py, logger.py. remaining_count 3? But only 2 files remain... maybe remaining_count is count of requirements. Also requests_left 4.\n\nI need to edit logger.py (NewLineFormatter + _setup_logger) and stats_logger.py (logger.info(m) → extra={\"keep_full_message\": True}).\n\nNeed current hash for logger.py: 5b8f8d9947de692353609c1fc2851097d24f9b6ba6004dce1dbfa394dc6d3ec8. stats_logger hash: 9ef93c3930d6dd8d889d4ba69558a85459c2210ae0714d5073c3b4328c8e7cb9.\n\nNote progress says environ.py changed already; I should verify what I added exists. It's already there per the read (int_or_none_min and probably DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS). Hmm, the ev of environ lines 1-150 shows int_or_none_min factory but I didn't see the actual env var (lines 1-150 may not include logging section). changed_files says environ.py changed, so presumably done.\n\nLet me make the logger.py edits now.\n\nFormatter format: need to truncate message after %s substitution before adding prefix. Approach: in format, compute args-substituted message without mutating record: msg_text, args = record.msg, record.args; if args: msg_text = str(record.msg) % record.args — but careful with non-dict args; logging does: if record.args and ... Actually logging.Formatter.format: it sets record.message = record.getMessage() then s = self.formatMessage(record). We can do:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\nsuper().format sets record.message to full substituted message. Then truncation would need us to rebuild the formatted string without touching shared record. Safer: compute budget-aware message first:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = self._truncate(message)\n        if truncated != message:\n            original_msg, original_args = record.msg, record.args\n            record.msg, record.args = truncated, None\n            try:\n                return self._format_with_prefix(record)\n            finally:\n                record.msg, record.args = original_msg, original_args\n    return self._format_with_prefix(record)\n```\n\nBut the spec says cannot rewrite shared record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. Temporarily swapping and restoring in finally is risky if exception; the test might check that record.msg unchanged after format — restoring does that. But safer: avoid touching record at all. We can produce formatted output by using the formatter's own machinery with a copy? logging.Formatter.format uses record attributes via %-style with record.__dict__. We could make a shallow copy of the record via `copy.copy(record)` but copy module not in allowed imports... Actually imports allowed list is for action code, not for business source. Business source can import copy. But spec says don't rewrite shared record — copy is fine. However simpler: override formatMessage to use a temporary message? Hmm.\n\nAlternative approach: compute formatted prefix separately. The format is _FORMAT which uses %(message)s. Simplest clean approach: create a shallow copy of record with copy.copy, set copy.msg=truncated, copy.args=(), then call super().format(copy). But super().format would also compute record.message etc on copy; and exc_info/stack_info preserved on copy. Also other custom attributes (keep_full_message) copied. That's clean. Wait: Formatter.format on copy sets copy.message; fine.\n\nBut careful: copying a LogRecord with copy.copy copies __dict__ shallowly — fine.\n\nHowever the multiline handling uses record.message from the copy; in our format method after super().format(record) we use record.message. Let's restructure:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if <limit applies>:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        limited = self._limit(message)\n        if limited != message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = limited\n            record.args = ()\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nWait but super().format(record) with record.msg full and no args -> message = limited. record.message = limited. Multiline prefix logic uses record.message → good.\n\nCareful: copy.copy retains exc_info yes.\n\nTruncation math: marker = \"...<omitted>...\" len 15. If len(message) <= budget return. remain = budget - 15. head = ceil(remain/2) = (remain+1)//2, tail = remain - head. Return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\"). If remain <= 0? budget>=32 so remain>=17; fine.\n\nmax_message_chars validation: must be int, not bool, >= 32, or None. else ValueError.\n\n_setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS — need import envs from deepinfer.environ in logger.py. Check for circular import: environ.py imports only stdlib. logger.py importing deepinfer.environ at module level... environ imports nothing from logger, so fine. But logger.py imports at top; environ.py might import others? Only stdlib from what we saw. Use local import inside _setup_logger to be safe? Module-level import is fine but local import avoids potential cycles. I'll do module-level `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — hmm, deepinfer package __init__ may import things causing cycle. Safer: inside _setup_logger do `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Do that.\n\nNow let's check that environ.py already has the entry: need to verify. Let me read environ.py around logging section, and then edit logger.py in the same action? Better: first check environ, then edit. Budget: 4 requests left. Combine: read environ section + apply logger edits in one action? Edits to logger.py don't depend on environ read except knowing the attribute name, which I'll set myself. But if environ is wrong I need to fix.\n\nLet me do: read environ env var section (search via search_code for \"LOG_MAX_MESSAGE\"), and edit logger.py in the same action.\n\nActually order: search first, print; then edits. Fine — single python block with both.\n\nFor stats_logger: logger.info(m) → logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True}).\n\nLet me write the logger edits carefully with replace_text.\n\nOld text 1 (init):\n```\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n```\nNew:\n```\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None and (\n            isinstance(max_message_chars, bool)\n            or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int)\n            or max_message_chars < _MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET\n        ):\n            raise ValueError(\n                f\"max_message_chars must be an